  "generated_text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( f(x) = x(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 3) \\), we start by rewriting the expression in a more manageable form. Notice that:\n\n\\[ f(x) = x(x + 3)(x + 1)(x + 2) = (x^2 + 3x)(x^2 + 3x + 2). \\]\n\nLet \\( y = x^2 + 3x \\). Then the function can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[ f(x) = y(y + 2). \\]\n\nWe need to find the minimum value of \\( g(y) = y(y + 2) = y^2 + 2y \\). This is a quadratic function in \\( y \\) and it opens upwards (since the coefficient of \\( y^2 \\) is positive), so its minimum value occurs at the vertex. The vertex of a parabola \\( ay^2 + by + c \\) is at \\( y = -\\frac{b}{2a} \\). Here, \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = 2 \\), so the vertex is at:\n\n\\[ y = -\\frac{2}{2 \\cdot 1} = -1. \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( y = -1 \\) into the quadratic function, we get:\n\n\\[ g(-1) = (-1)^2 + 2(-1) = 1 - 2 = -1. \\]\n\nSo, the minimum value of \\( g(y) \\) is \\(-1\\). Now, we need to determine if this minimum value can actually be achieved by the original function \\( f(x) \\). We solve the equation \\( y = x^2 + 3x = -1 \\):\n\n\\[ x^2 + 3x + 1 = 0. \\]\n\nThe solutions to this quadratic equation are:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{5}}{2}. \\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\( f(x) \\) is indeed achieved at these points. Therefore, the minimum value of \\( x(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 3) \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{-1}. \\]",
